---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const link = (path = "") => `${base}${base.endsWith("/") ? "" : "/"}${path.replace(/^\//, "")}`;
---

<Base title="Uses — tinylion1024" description="The tools tinylion1024 currently uses and the principles behind keeping them." current="/en/uses" lang="en" languageUrl="/uses" alternates={[{ lang: "zh-CN", href: "/uses" }, { lang: "en", href: "/en/uses" }]}>
  <section class="essay about">
    <div class="eyebrow">USES / MINIMAL TOOLSET</div>
    <h1>Tools should remove friction, not manufacture identity.</h1>
    <p class="dek">The small set I actually use today, expected to change with the work.</p>
    <div class="essay-rule"></div>
    <div class="uses-list">
      <section><h2>Terminal</h2><p><strong>Warp</strong> for AI collaboration, visual context, and complex tasks; <strong>Ghostty</strong> for Git, tests, logs, and SSH.</p></section>
      <section><h2>Workspace</h2><p><strong>tmux / Zellij</strong> for session recovery and task boundaries. A pane represents responsibility, not another agent by default.</p></section>
      <section><h2>AI Coding</h2><p><strong>Codex / Claude Code</strong>, selected by task. Small changes stay direct; high-risk work earns planning, tests, and review.</p></section>
      <section><h2>Publishing</h2><p><strong>Astro, TypeScript, pnpm, and GitHub Pages</strong>. Static, reviewable, low-maintenance, and enough for durable writing.</p></section>
    </div>
    <a class="text-link" href={link("en/posts/choosing-a-terminal-warp-ghostty-iterm2")}>Read how I choose a terminal →</a>
  </section>
</Base>
